### 3.3 Authenticated release request and immutable candidate preparation

`release-prepare.yml` triggers only on
`repository_dispatch: types: [relux_release_request]`. GitHub loads this
workflow from the default branch and supplies the last default-branch commit as
`GITHUB_SHA`/`GITHUB_REF`; there is no tag/push trigger, selectable-ref
`workflow_dispatch`, or upstream workflow whose code or output becomes
authority. The request payload contains only a request ID, intended semantic
version, and candidate commit. Every payload field is untrusted data.

The external request client uses a short-lived, repository-scoped GitHub App
installation token. GitHub currently requires `Contents: write` to create a
repository dispatch event, so the credential is not described as read-only.
It has no Actions, Workflows, Administration, Environments, Secrets, Checks, or
publication permission; the App is not a protected-branch or protected-tag
ruleset bypass actor. The token is held outside repository Actions, is never
sent in the event payload or exposed to the workflow, calls only the GitHub
repository-dispatch endpoint, and produces a request/audit receipt. Compromise
can enqueue or replay requests and modify only refs/content allowed by ordinary
repository rules; it cannot make an unprotected commit eligible, create the
protected release tag, approve an environment, obtain production secrets, or
publish. Release owns initiation; recorded engineering/security/release
approvals remain separate and mandatory at `release/review-gate`.

The first job requires the expected repository, event type, GitHub App
installation/sender, unique request ID, `github.ref` equal to the protected
default branch, and `github.workflow_sha` equal to the protected default-branch
workflow revision whose file and policy SHA-256 digests are checked in. It then
resolves the requested commit independently and requires it to be an exact
commit reachable from the protected default branch. `release/revalidate` then
runs all nine checks fresh for that exact candidate.
The intended `v*` tag must be absent and unreserved; it is created atomically at
the accepted candidate commit only by the later protected publication job.
Missing, replayed, ambiguous, moved, or inconsistent metadata is `unknown` and
stops before candidate acceptance.

Every credential-capable reusable workflow is called as
`owner/repository/.github/workflows/<file>.yml@<full-reviewed-commit-SHA>` and
that SHA plus file digest appears in the immutable candidate. Relative local
workflow calls and tag/branch refs are prohibited for release authority because
they resolve from mutable or caller-selected code.

| Job / blocking check | Actor trust and input | Token / environment / secrets | Network | Outputs / retention | Approval |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `release/candidate-identity` | Default-branch `repository_dispatch`; independently validate sender/request and resolve the untrusted candidate SHA/version; exact default-branch-reachable commit, absent intended tag, trusted workflow SHA and policy digest | `contents: read`; no production environment; no secrets; request credential is external and unavailable | GitHub repository/ref metadata reads; public egress exists but no other endpoint is authorized | Immutable candidate manifest, request identity, intended tag/version, commit ancestry, and workflow-provenance evidence; release-record/7y | Release-owner initiation; not release approval |
| `release/revalidate` | Exact candidate manifest | `contents: read`; no production environment; no secrets | Same allowlists as credential-free CI | Fresh results for every active required check, with digests | None |
| `release/review-gate` | Exact manifest plus complete active evidence index | `contents: read`; no production environment; no secrets | Public egress exists; no network use is authorized | Machine verdict: promotable/non-promotable/unknown; release-record/7y | Recorded engineering/security/release approvals; human ratification remains `TASK-260717-2d308k` before publication |

The candidate manifest records request identity, source commit, clean-state assertion, source and
dependency locks, toolchain/SDK/runner images, protocol and relay manifests,
marketing/build versions, intended absent tag, expected artifact names, the
trusted caller workflow SHA/policy digest, every reusable-workflow SHA/file
digest, and every evidence digest. It is immutable after
`release/review-gate` starts.

