@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+# TASK-260715-whtdsf — GitHub issue-trigger trust research (rework 6)
+
+Checked: 2026-08-30
+
+Question: Can the release-request caller trigger trusted default-branch
+workflow code without receiving a repository permission that also authorizes
+Git refs or GitHub Release/asset publication?
+
+## Official evidence
+
+- The `issues` workflow event uses the last commit and ref of the default
+  branch and triggers only when the workflow file exists on that default
+  branch. Source:
+  https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/events-that-trigger-workflows#issues
+- Creating an issue with a fine-grained token requires `Issues: write`.
+  Source:
+  https://docs.github.com/en/rest/issues/issues#create-an-issue
+- Creating `repository_dispatch` requires `Contents: write`.
+  Source:
+  https://docs.github.com/en/rest/repos/repos#create-a-repository-dispatch-event
+- `Contents: write` also authorizes create/update/delete GitHub Release
+  operations, update/delete release assets, and create/update/delete Git refs.
+  Sources:
+  https://docs.github.com/en/rest/releases/releases
+  https://docs.github.com/en/rest/releases/assets
+  https://docs.github.com/en/rest/git/refs
+
+## Binding conclusion
+
+`repository_dispatch` cannot provide the claimed requester boundary because its
+minimum token overlaps release/asset/ref publication APIs. Use one strict
+`issues: opened` request event instead. The external GitHub App has only
+`Issues: write`; the workflow validates the App sender and immutable captured
+event digest under trusted default-branch code. The issue contents remain
+untrusted and a refetched/edited issue never becomes authority. Negative tests
+must prove issue-plane mutation is possible while Contents, refs,
+Releases/assets, Actions/workflows, environments, and secrets are denied.
+
